Day 2----Understanding the topics clearly
STACK POINTER:
Stack: it is a memory used to store temporary information.For ex,if u assign the plates horizontally,you cannot remove the second plate directly,first you have to remove the plates on top of it..this is called LIFO(last in first out)
Later I understood what stack pointer is..imagine the cpu has stored several data in stack,now how will the cpu gets to know where is the top of the stack..it uses register called stack pointer…it helps the cpu to point the address of the data and tell it the location of that data
SP is incremented first, then the data is stored.
PUSH:put data into stack, if the SP=07H a push happens and the SP becomes08H and data is stored in 08H
POP:remove data from stack,if SP=08H and the data is stored in 08H a pop removes that data so the SP moves back to 07H
And also the SP is important for storing return information
This is all about stack pointer


Day 3--- Status/Flag Information:
So basically the cpu takes the instruction,processes the data and produce the output…the cpu needs to know what happened during operation..so to know these condition the cpu uses flag
FLAG: it is a 1 bit indicator,the cpu changes flag based on the what happens during execution.
So basically our simulator needs to behave like the real MS51FB9AE CPU.so if an instruction changes a flag, your simulator needs to update that flag too.


Day 4---Memory organization
So the memory is not used for only one purpose…the memory is divided into 2 types..program memory and data memory
Program memory stores the program instruction
Data memory is used to store the data of the program
RAM is memory that the CPU can use while the program is running.
Registers are very small and very fast storage locations inside the CPU/microcontroller.
